WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.130 --> 00:00:04.730
Film Courage: Your movie FOR THE LOVE OF GEORGE,
did you intend to have the crowdfunding campaign

00:00:04.730 --> 00:00:09.170
be the sole source of production funds or
did you have other ideas?

00:00:09.170 --> 00:00:10.650
Nadia Jordan, director, producer, actor: No.

00:00:10.650 --> 00:00:15.219
The crowdfunding campaign at the start…I
didn’t know if I was going to make a short

00:00:15.219 --> 00:00:16.509
or a feature.

00:00:16.509 --> 00:00:20.470
So we did the crowdfunding campaign and kind
of just threw it out there to see what would

00:00:20.470 --> 00:00:25.919
happen and it was 7 weeks of extremely hard
work.

00:00:25.919 --> 00:00:31.419
And at the end of it we got a certain amount
of money and it was a lot of money in which

00:00:31.419 --> 00:00:34.450
to make a short, but not near enough in which
to make a feature.

00:00:34.450 --> 00:00:39.840
But at that point I didn’t want to make
a short and waste it on a short.

00:00:39.840 --> 00:00:44.610
So many people I know were saying “You can’t
make a feature, your first feature.

00:00:44.610 --> 00:00:46.940
You have to make a short first.”

00:00:46.940 --> 00:00:49.920
What rule book says anywhere that you have
to make a short first?

00:00:49.920 --> 00:00:55.350
And so many people that I knew had made a
short and regretted it and they are now using

00:00:55.350 --> 00:00:57.900
that as a funding pitch for a feature.

00:00:57.900 --> 00:01:03.000
And so at that point I thought I really want
to make a feature, you know?

00:01:03.000 --> 00:01:04.649
Let’s go after that.

00:01:04.649 --> 00:01:07.420
And this script was worked on and got better
and better.

00:01:07.420 --> 00:01:12.310
And the more it improved the more I wanted
to make a better and better feature and wanted

00:01:12.310 --> 00:01:13.310
to wait.

00:01:13.310 --> 00:01:19.409
So yeah, it was then something that I then
had to look outside of crowdfunding and start

00:01:19.409 --> 00:01:23.689
having meetings and pitching people and like
I say, everyone in Los Angeles is trying to

00:01:23.689 --> 00:01:24.859
do that.

00:01:24.859 --> 00:01:30.439
It’s fairly easy to get in doors in LA because
everyone is worried about missing the next

00:01:30.439 --> 00:01:31.740
best thing.

00:01:31.740 --> 00:01:39.539
It’s a lot harder to get anyone to commit
or get funding or that side of it.

00:01:39.539 --> 00:01:41.240
I really tried everything.

00:01:41.240 --> 00:01:48.320
Like I would go to investors and film financiers
in LA and I had a few interesting meetings

00:01:48.320 --> 00:01:52.610
where they would want to change the script
and they wanted to change the husband and

00:01:52.610 --> 00:01:56.539
the husband should be…(this was one male
financier that I went to see) had very strong

00:01:56.539 --> 00:02:01.319
ideas about how Poppy [the protagonist in
FOR THE LOVE OF GEORGE] toward her husband

00:02:01.319 --> 00:02:05.090
which I didn’t listen to at all and he didn’t
finance the film.

00:02:05.090 --> 00:02:09.400
But eventually you try everything.

00:02:09.400 --> 00:02:13.340
I was getting all of these creative ideas
about how I was going to do funding and I

00:02:13.340 --> 00:02:18.540
was going to go to the country club and fundraising
meetings and talk to people about how they

00:02:18.540 --> 00:02:22.700
can get involved in film, which I actually
tried doing that.

00:02:22.700 --> 00:02:23.700
None of it worked.

00:02:23.700 --> 00:02:29.670
But I was really trying to get creative and
go outside of the box in terms of getting

00:02:29.670 --> 00:02:33.780
money within LA because at that time I decided
I didn’t want to give the rights up.

00:02:33.780 --> 00:02:36.970
I didn’t want to have someone else make
it through their production company.

00:02:36.970 --> 00:02:41.430
I wanted to have someone give me money to
make it through my production company.

00:02:41.430 --> 00:02:45.902
It was a bit further down the line I realized
again ‘Well, if I want this I’m going

00:02:45.902 --> 00:02:51.050
to try and have to get the money myself,’
and that’s what I did.

00:02:51.050 --> 00:02:55.730
Film Courage: Was this from a series of investors
(aside from the crowdfunding)?

00:02:55.730 --> 00:03:01.370
Nadia Jordan: Yeah, I think we had about 4
or 5 investors.

00:03:01.370 --> 00:03:07.080
And you know, it was really about relationship
building and finding people who could relate

00:03:07.080 --> 00:03:16.150
to either the subject or friends of other
investors or things like that.

00:03:16.150 --> 00:03:22.610
It was a very long process and very time-consuming
and I can’t tell you the amount of emails

00:03:22.610 --> 00:03:29.220
I sent out (thousands) trying to find ways
to raise the money.

00:03:29.220 --> 00:03:34.790
But I was very fortunate in the end it all
came together and I had a couple of actually

00:03:34.790 --> 00:03:39.992
females in particular that believed in the
project and believed in me and yeah, I’m

00:03:39.992 --> 00:03:41.870
very grateful for that.

00:03:41.870 --> 00:03:45.800
Film Courage: I almost envision Poppy’s
character in sort of the situation you were

00:03:45.800 --> 00:03:50.770
going through in that everybody is in her
ear kind of like telling her what to do and

00:03:50.770 --> 00:03:53.660
how it should be, I find that interesting.

00:03:53.660 --> 00:03:57.362
How did you decipher “Okay, I’m going
to listen to this person.

00:03:57.362 --> 00:04:01.319
This person is probably very knowledgable
and I’m sure they have great intentions,

00:04:01.319 --> 00:04:03.820
but I don’t think this is for me.”

00:04:03.820 --> 00:04:05.730
How did you know whom to listen to?

00:04:05.730 --> 00:04:12.260
Nadia Jordan: I think I just have a very strong
belief if you want to do something that badly

00:04:12.260 --> 00:04:16.799
and not something that is completely delusional,
than you can achieve it.

00:04:16.799 --> 00:04:19.860
And I had people who said “Oh, you know
you can’t do a film about George Clooney

00:04:19.860 --> 00:04:21.989
unless you have a budget of 10 million.

00:04:21.989 --> 00:04:22.989
And I’m like…what?

00:04:22.989 --> 00:04:26.550
Sometimes I just think where do these people
get this stuff from?

00:04:26.550 --> 00:04:32.030
Who taught them that because it’s just blatantly
not true.

00:04:32.030 --> 00:04:37.650
I don’t know, I’m just a believer that
if you really put everything into it unless

00:04:37.650 --> 00:04:41.690
you have some really random, stupid idea,
than why can’t you do it?

00:04:41.690 --> 00:04:45.860
You know, who is to tell me I can’t and
if I’m not getting anywhere than maybe I’ll

00:04:45.860 --> 00:04:47.690
rethink it, but let me try it.

00:04:47.690 --> 00:04:48.990
Let me try doing it my way.

